  "notes": "Dutch trance-pop — the bridge from Eurodance into vocal trance — and three imported trance conventions are what separate it from the 1994 Eurodance cohort. First, \"a de-emphasised kick to leave room for the bassline\": the accent tier drops to 104-118, well under the hands-up models here, while the bass runs continuously. Second, \"a regular open hi-hat on the upbeat\": the open hat is near-certain on all four offbeats at 4 per bar. Third, \"lengthy snare rolls building in velocity and volume into the drop\", encoded as a 36-124 ramp with almost no gap or descend probability — a single unbroken climb. The melodic side follows the entry too: \"a single sustained saw lead playing a long, simple, singable anthem melody is the hook; loops are 8 or 16 bars rather than 2 or 4\", so the melody is a low-density legato long arc over 8- and 16-bar phrases, and the bass is \"a rolling 16th-note trance bass sitting under a de-emphasised kick — the low end is continuous rather than punctual\". ⚠ The research marks the key and drum grammar `[LOW-CONF]` as trance-convention rather than act documentation; that mark is repeated here, and every velocity and density above is INFERRED.",
